00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:33.900
Imagine you bought a high growth US tech stock in your TFSA last month at $48.50, and it rallies 18% by year end.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:43.400
The contribution room you used stays intact, but the entire gain compounds tax-free inside the account rather than triggering capital gains tax outside it.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:51.260
The mechanics hinge on annual contribution limits that reset each January, with unused room carrying forward indefinitely.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:56.800
Yet over contributions trigger a 1% monthly penalty on the excess until withdrawn.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:08.260
Professionals always verify the exact unused room via the CRA's My Account Portal before allocating new capital, especially when shifting from cash holdings into equities.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:20.660
US dividend withholding tax still applies at 15% even inside a TFSA, so Canadian investors often favor growth names or Canadian listed ETFs to minimize that drag.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:26.000
The common misconception is treating the TFSA like a high interest savings account.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:35.700
The fix is to allocate the full contribution room to assets with the highest expected after tax compounding potential, given your risk tolerance and time horizon.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:50.160
Another layer is the superficial loss rule that still applies inside the TFSA, meaning an investor cannot immediately repurchase a security sold at a loss and expect the loss to reduce future taxable gains outside the account.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:09:01.840
Tracking adjusted cost-base becomes simpler inside the TFSA because all gains and losses stay sheltered, freeing mental bandwidth for security selection rather than tax accounting.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:18.120
For investors with $50,000 or more in-room, allocating half to a broad Canadian equity ETF and half to a concentrated growth name has historically produced stronger long-term outcomes than parking the entire balance in cash or GICs.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:27.900
The annual reset also creates a natural January rebalancing window where investors can top up without worrying about contribution limits being exceeded mid-year.

